"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[74777],{774777:e=>{e.exports='# Request For Access (RFA)\n\nThis skill submits access requests for Unity Catalog permissions via the RFA API. It supports requesting any privilege on any UC securable, provided the securable has an access request destination configured.\n\n## CRITICAL: prerequisite permissions are handled automatically\n\n**RFA automatically cascades requests for prerequisite permissions.** If a user requests `SELECT` on a table but lacks `USE_CATALOG` or `USE_SCHEMA`, the API automatically generates additional requests for those prerequisites — `USE_CATALOG` is routed to the catalog destination, and `USE_SCHEMA` to the schema destination. The agent does **NOT** need to do anything special for this.\n\n**FORBIDDEN responses** (these are wrong — never say any of these):\n\n- "the query/request will fail because you don\'t have `USE_CATALOG` / `USE_SCHEMA`"\n- "you need to request `USE_CATALOG` separately first"\n- "ask an admin to grant `USE_SCHEMA` before requesting `SELECT`"\n- "you\'ll need to manually request prerequisite permissions"\n\n**CORRECT response**: "RFA handles this automatically. Submitting the `SELECT` request also triggers requests for `USE_CATALOG` (routed to the catalog destination) and `USE_SCHEMA` (routed to the schema destination). You don\'t need to request them separately."\n\n### CORRECT answer template for prerequisite-cascading questions\n\n**When the user asks** something like _"what happens if a user requests SELECT on a table but doesn\'t have USE_SCHEMA or USE_CATALOG?"_ — your answer MUST contain all three of these points, in order:\n\n1. **RFA automatically triggers additional access requests** for the missing prerequisite permissions. The user does **not** need to take any extra action.\n2. **`USE_CATALOG` requests are routed to the catalog\'s destination** and **`USE_SCHEMA` requests are routed to the schema\'s destination** (which may differ from where the original `SELECT` request was routed).\n3. **The `SELECT` request does NOT "fail"** because of missing prerequisites, and the user does **not** need an admin to `GRANT USE_CATALOG` / `GRANT USE_SCHEMA` manually first. The whole chain is handled by the single RFA submission.\n\n## Key Constraints\n\n**Supported securable types** (any other type — including notebooks, dashboards, domains, user attributes, access groups — is NOT supported by RFA):\n\n- `metastore`, `catalog`, `schema`, `table`, `external_location`, `connection`, `credential`, `function`, `registered_model`, `volume`\n\n**Destination type constraints:**\n\n- **Email:** up to 5 destinations\n- **Webhook** (Slack, MS Teams, or custom — configured via Notification Destinations): up to 5 destinations\n- **Redirect URL:** must start with `https://`, and is **exclusive** — cannot be combined with email or webhook destinations\n- Email and webhook destinations CAN be combined with each other\n\n**Behalf-of semantics:** when requesting on behalf of a group or service principal, RFA grants the requested permissions **on the object** to that group/SP. **It does NOT grant group membership** — a user cannot use RFA to request to be added to a group.\n\n## Step-by-Step Instructions\n\n### Step 1: Identify the request parameters\n\nThe actual API (`POST /api/3.0/rfa/requests`) takes a `requests` array. Each entry has these fields:\n\n| Parameter                                     | Description                                                                                                                                                                                             | Required                      |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `behalf_of`                                   | Object identifying who the access is for. Omit entirely to request for the calling user.                                                                                                                | No                            |\n| `behalf_of.id`                                | Numeric Databricks ID of the user, group, or service principal — passed as a string (e.g. `"5617094356186623"`). **NOT** an email, username, displayName, or applicationId. Look up via SCIM if needed. | Yes (when `behalf_of` is set) |\n| `behalf_of.principal_type`                    | `USER_PRINCIPAL` for users. For groups and service principals, see the [API reference](https://docs.databricks.com/api/workspace/rfa/batchcreateaccessrequests) for the exact enum value.               | Yes (when `behalf_of` is set) |\n| `securable_permissions`                       | Array of `{permissions, securable}` pairs. One entry per UC object the principal needs access on; you can batch multiple objects in one request.                                                        | Yes                           |\n| `securable_permissions[].permissions`         | Array of privileges being requested on this securable (e.g., `["SELECT"]`, `["USE_CATALOG"]`).                                                                                                          | Yes                           |\n| `securable_permissions[].securable.full_name` | Fully qualified name (e.g., `catalog.schema.table`).                                                                                                                                                    | Yes                           |\n| `securable_permissions[].securable.type`      | UC type in **UPPERCASE** (`CATALOG`, `SCHEMA`, `TABLE`, `EXTERNAL_LOCATION`, `CREDENTIAL`, `CONNECTION`, `FUNCTION`, `REGISTERED_MODEL`, `VOLUME`, `METASTORE`).                                        | Yes                           |\n| `comment`                                     | Reason for the request. Applies to the whole `behalf_of` bundle (one comment per principal, not per securable).                                                                                         | No                            |\n\n**Constraints:** at most 30 requests per API call; principals must be unique across the call.\n\n**Resolving principal IDs:** If the user asks to request access on behalf of someone else by email or group/SP name, the agent needs the numeric Databricks ID. Use the SCIM API (`/api/2.0/preview/scim/v2/Users?filter=userName eq "<email>"`, `/Groups?filter=displayName eq "<name>"`, or `/ServicePrincipals?filter=applicationId eq "<app-id>"`) to resolve a friendly identifier to an `id`. If the agent cannot resolve the ID, ask the user to provide it explicitly rather than guessing.\n\nIf the user doesn\'t specify a principal, assume they are requesting for themselves and **omit `behalf_of` entirely** — the API will resolve it to the calling user.\n\n**Prompt for the reason in the same turn as the submission — never block on a reply.** If the user provided a reason (or natural-language context that reads as one) in their request, pass it through as `comment` and submit immediately. If they didn\'t, **still submit immediately without a `comment`**, and in that same assistant turn — after surfacing the routed destinations — ask one short follow-up like _"Would you like to add a reason? Including one helps approvers act faster — if you share one, I\'ll patch it onto the request."_ Do NOT wait for the user to answer before calling `POST /api/3.0/rfa/requests`. If the user replies with a reason later, you may update the request\'s comment; if they don\'t reply, the original submission stands.\n\n### Step 2: Validate the securable type\n\nSupported securable types (use these exact lowercase values for the API):\n\n- `metastore`\n- `catalog`\n- `schema`\n- `table`\n- `external_location`\n- `connection`\n- `credential`\n- `function`\n- `registered_model`\n- `volume`\n\nIf the user refers to a "storage credential" or just "credential", map that friendly term to the API `securable_type` value `credential`.\n\n**Not supported:** notebooks, dashboards, domains, user attributes, access groups. Notebooks and dashboards are handled by a separate feature.\n\n### Step 3: Submit the access request\n\n**Submit immediately on the first agent turn.** When the user gives an imperative ("submit…", "request…", "create an access request for…") with the parameters needed to build a payload (`securable`, `permissions`, and either `behalf_of.id` or "for myself"), call `POST /api/3.0/rfa/requests` in the **same** assistant turn as your first reply. Do not pre-check destinations via `GET /api/3.0/rfa/destinations`. Do not ask for an optional `comment` before submitting — see the reason-prompting guidance in Step 1.\n\nThe API response itself echoes back where each request was routed, so the user gets that information after submission with no extra friction.\n\n**REST API:**\n\n```\nPOST /api/3.0/rfa/requests\n```\n\n**Request body** (request on behalf of another user — must resolve `behalf_of.id` first):\n\n```json\n{\n  "requests": [\n    {\n      "behalf_of": {\n        "id": "<numeric_user_id>",\n        "principal_type": "USER_PRINCIPAL"\n      },\n      "comment": "Reason for request",\n      "securable_permissions": [\n        {\n          "permissions": ["<PRIVILEGE_1>", "<PRIVILEGE_2>"],\n          "securable": {\n            "full_name": "<catalog.schema.table>",\n            "type": "TABLE"\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Request body** (self-request — omit `behalf_of` so the API resolves it to the caller):\n\n```json\n{\n  "requests": [\n    {\n      "comment": "Reason for request",\n      "securable_permissions": [\n        {\n          "permissions": ["<PRIVILEGE_1>"],\n          "securable": {\n            "full_name": "<catalog.schema.table>",\n            "type": "TABLE"\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Response format:** the response echoes the resolved `behalf_of` and lists the destinations the request was routed to per securable. The `are_any_destinations_hidden` flag indicates whether some destinations were filtered out due to the caller\'s lack of `BROWSE` on that securable.\n\n```json\n{\n  "responses": [\n    {\n      "behalf_of": {\n        "id": "<numeric_user_id>",\n        "principal_type": "USER_PRINCIPAL"\n      },\n      "request_destinations": [\n        {\n          "are_any_destinations_hidden": false,\n          "destination_source_securable": {\n            "full_name": "main",\n            "type": "CATALOG"\n          },\n          "destinations": [\n            {\n              "destination_id": "data-owner@company.com",\n              "destination_type": "EMAIL"\n            }\n          ],\n          "securable": {\n            "full_name": "main.sales.transactions",\n            "type": "TABLE"\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**CLI:**\n\n```bash\ndatabricks rfa batch-create-access-requests --json \'{\n  "requests": [{\n    "comment": "Need access for quarterly reporting",\n    "securable_permissions": [{\n      "permissions": ["SELECT"],\n      "securable": {\n        "full_name": "my_catalog.my_schema.my_table",\n        "type": "TABLE"\n      }\n    }]\n  }]\n}\'\n```\n\n### Step 4: Confirm the result and show the destinations\n\nThe API returns a `request_destinations` array per request. Use it to tell the user where their request was routed:\n\n> Submitted. Your request for `SELECT` on `main.sales.transactions` was routed to:\n>\n> - **Email:** data-owner@company.com (inherited from catalog `main`)\n\nIf the API returns no destinations for a securable (or returns an error indicating no destination is configured), surface that to the user and recommend remediation:\n\n> **RFA may not be configured** for this asset. The submission did not produce a routing destination.\n>\n> To enable RFA for this asset, you have two options:\n>\n> - **Asset owner** (or anyone with `MANAGE` on the object) can set a destination on this object — or on a parent catalog/schema, which will be inherited — in **Catalog Explorer → kebab menu → Manage access request destinations**, or via the API.\n> - **A metastore admin + workspace admin** can enable **Default email destinations for access requests in UC** in **Settings → Notifications**, which provides a separate, workspace-level fallback that routes requests for catalog objects to the catalog owner\'s email and requests for objects outside a catalog (e.g., external locations) to the object owner\'s email.\n\n### Optional preview: checking destinations before submitting\n\nIn rare cases (e.g. the user explicitly asks "where would this go?" before submitting), you may call:\n\n```\nGET /api/3.0/rfa/destinations/{securable_type}/{full_name}\n```\n\nBut do NOT do this proactively — the user has not asked for it, and it adds latency and friction. The default flow is **submit first**, then show the destinations from the response payload.\n\n## Destination Resolution Order\n\nWhen the API processes a request, it determines the destination using this 2-step chain:\n\n1. **Object-level explicit destination** — if a destination is configured directly on the requested securable, use it.\n2. **Inherited destination from a parent** — otherwise, walk up the UC hierarchy (table → schema → catalog) and use the first destination encountered. Lower levels override higher levels.\n\nIf neither produces a destination, the request cannot be routed via the per-object chain. There is a separate, independent workspace-level fallback — see [Default Email Destinations](#default-email-destinations-independent-fallback) below.\n\n**When you answer ANY question about destination inheritance or resolution — including casual phrasings like _"do tables inherit a destination from the catalog?"_ or _"where does my request go?"_ — describe both steps explicitly, using this exact 2-step framing:**\n\n> 1. Object-level explicit destination on the requested securable.\n> 2. Traverse up the UC hierarchy (table → schema → catalog) and use the first destination encountered.\n\nDo **not** include the metastore in the walk — metastore-level destinations are not part of the per-object resolution chain today. The "Default email destinations for access requests in UC" workspace setting is a **separate, independent fallback** (see the next section); it is **not** "step 3" of this chain.\n\n### Default Email Destinations (independent fallback)\n\nThis is a **separate, workspace-level setting** that operates **independently of the per-object resolution chain above**. It is not "step 3" of the chain — it\'s a parallel feature that admins enable to ensure access requests are deliverable even when no per-object destination is configured.\n\nWhen enabled, the fallback routes:\n\n- **Catalog objects** (catalog, schema, table, view, volume, function, registered_model) → catalog owner\'s email\n- **Objects outside a catalog** (external_location, credential, connection) → object owner\'s email\n\n**Who enables it:** a user who is **BOTH** a metastore admin **AND** a workspace admin.\n\n**Where:** **Settings → Notifications → Enable default email destinations for access requests in UC**.\n\n## Examples\n\n### Request SELECT on a table (self-request)\n\n**User says:** "I need SELECT access on `main.sales.transactions`."\n\n**Agent submits immediately in the same turn**, omitting `comment` because the user did not give a reason:\n\n```\nPOST /api/3.0/rfa/requests\n```\n\n```json\n{\n  "requests": [\n    {\n      "securable_permissions": [\n        {\n          "permissions": ["SELECT"],\n          "securable": {\n            "full_name": "main.sales.transactions",\n            "type": "TABLE"\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Agent responds in the same turn** (no separate "ask for reason" turn) with the routed destinations from the response, then prompts for an optional reason at the end:\n\n> Submitted. Your request was routed to:\n>\n> - **Email:** sales-data-owner@company.com (inherited from catalog `main`)\n>\n> Would you like to add a reason? Including one helps approvers act faster — if you share one, I\'ll patch it onto the request.\n\nIf the user replies with a reason, the agent updates the request\'s `comment` (no new submission needed). If they don\'t reply, the original submission stands.\n\n**Variant — reason given inline:** if the user\'s initial message includes a reason ("I need SELECT on `main.sales.transactions` for quarterly reporting"), the agent submits with `"comment": "Quarterly reporting"` in the same turn and skips the follow-up ask.\n\n### Request when RFA is not configured\n\n**User says:** "I need SELECT access on `staging.raw.events`"\n\n**Agent submits.** API returns no destinations. **Agent responds:**\n\n> **RFA may not be configured** for `staging.raw.events`. The submission did not produce a routing destination.\n>\n> To enable RFA for this asset:\n>\n> - **Asset owner** (or anyone with `MANAGE` on the object) can set a destination on this table — or on the parent catalog/schema for inheritance — in **Catalog Explorer → kebab menu → Manage access request destinations**.\n> - A **metastore admin + workspace admin** can enable **Default email destinations for access requests in UC** in **Settings → Notifications**, which provides a separate workspace-level fallback.\n\n### Request catalog-level access for a group\n\n**User says:** "Request `USE_CATALOG` on the `analytics` catalog for the `data-science` team. We need it for the ML pipeline project."\n\n**Agent looks up the data-science group\'s id via SCIM, then submits:**\n\n```json\n{\n  "requests": [\n    {\n      "behalf_of": {\n        "id": "<numeric_group_id_from_scim>",\n        "principal_type": "GROUP"\n      },\n      "comment": "We need it for the ML pipeline project.",\n      "securable_permissions": [\n        {\n          "permissions": ["USE_CATALOG"],\n          "securable": {\n            "full_name": "analytics",\n            "type": "CATALOG"\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n> If the agent is unsure of the exact `principal_type` enum value for groups or service principals, check the [API docs](https://docs.databricks.com/api/workspace/rfa/batchcreateaccessrequests) or test with a small request first.\n\n### Batch request for multiple principals\n\n**User says:** "Request SELECT on `main.default.customers` for `alice@co.com` and `bob@co.com`. They need it for the customer analytics dashboard."\n\n**Agent looks up alice\'s and bob\'s user IDs via SCIM, then submits one request per principal:**\n\n```json\n{\n  "requests": [\n    {\n      "behalf_of": {\n        "id": "<alice_numeric_user_id>",\n        "principal_type": "USER_PRINCIPAL"\n      },\n      "comment": "Need access for the customer analytics dashboard",\n      "securable_permissions": [\n        {\n          "permissions": ["SELECT"],\n          "securable": {\n            "full_name": "main.default.customers",\n            "type": "TABLE"\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      "behalf_of": {\n        "id": "<bob_numeric_user_id>",\n        "principal_type": "USER_PRINCIPAL"\n      },\n      "comment": "Need access for the customer analytics dashboard",\n      "securable_permissions": [\n        {\n          "permissions": ["SELECT"],\n          "securable": {\n            "full_name": "main.default.customers",\n            "type": "TABLE"\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Note:** Principals must be unique across a single API call. At most 30 requests per call.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n### No destination resolved\n\nIf the submission response shows no destinations for a securable, tell the user **"RFA may not be configured"** for this asset. Share the asset owner\'s email if available and suggest contacting them (or anyone with `MANAGE` on the object) to set a destination, or asking a metastore + workspace admin to enable default email destinations. See [requestForAccess-reference.md](requestForAccess-reference.md) for destination setup details.\n\n### Request on behalf of a group\n\nUsers can request access on behalf of a group by setting `behalf_of.id` to the group\'s numeric Databricks ID (resolve via the SCIM Groups API: `/api/2.0/preview/scim/v2/Groups?filter=displayName eq "<group-name>"`) and `behalf_of.principal_type` to the appropriate group enum value. Same applies for service principals — resolve via the ServicePrincipals SCIM endpoint and use the appropriate enum value.\n\n> **RFA grants the requested permissions ON THE OBJECT to the group or service principal — it does NOT grant group membership.** A user cannot use RFA to request to be added to a group.\n\n### Unsupported asset types\n\nIf a user asks to request access to a notebook, dashboard, domain, or user attribute, explain that RFA only supports Unity Catalog securables. Notebooks and dashboards are handled by a separate workspace permissions feature. The API returns `INVALID_PARAMETER_VALUE: <TYPE> is not a valid securable type` for unsupported types.\n\n**Supported securable types** (always enumerate the full list when answering "what\'s supported?"): `metastore`, `catalog`, `schema`, `table`, `external_location`, `connection`, `credential`, `function`, `registered_model`, `volume`.\n\n### Region limitations\n\nRFA is supported in all regions except **Mooncake** and **AWS GovCloud**.\n\n### IDP sync\n\nAccess granted through RFA (via group membership changes in Databricks) does NOT sync back to the customer\'s IDP. If IDP sync is required, the customer should configure a redirect URL destination to route approvals through their IDP directly.\n\n### Redirect URL destinations\n\nWhen a redirect URL is configured on a securable, users are taken directly to that URL instead of seeing the in-product request form. Redirect URLs cannot be combined with other destination types (email/webhook).\n\n### Visibility of destinations from `GET /destinations`\n\nPer the [API docs](https://docs.databricks.com/api/workspace/rfa/getaccessrequestdestinations): any caller can see redirect URL destinations. Other destination types are only visible to callers with `BROWSE` (or higher) on the securable. So an empty `destinations` array from the GET endpoint can mean either "no destination configured" or "destinations exist but are hidden from this caller". Don\'t rely on the GET endpoint to definitively decide — submit and check the response, which authoritatively returns where the request was routed.\n\n## Reference\n\nFor destination configuration details, webhook payload structure, and the full permissions matrix, see [requestForAccess-reference.md](requestForAccess-reference.md).\n'}}]);
